ss=\"lex-cite-scope\">11</span> </span>. [<span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Zenod.</span> </span> and <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Aristarch.</span> </span> scanned <span class=\"gk\">Τροίη</span> (Troy) as disyll. everywhere in <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Hom.</span> </span>, but <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Aristarch.</span> </span> scanned (and wrote) (<span class=\"lex-etym gk\">πόλιν</span>)<span class=\"gk\"> Τροΐην</span> as trisyll. in <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Il.</span> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">1.129</span> </span>, <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Od.</span> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">11.510</span> </span>, understanding 'a Trojan city (city of the Troad)', <abbr>v.</abbr> Sch. <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Il.</span> </span> l.c.; cf. <span class=\"gk\">Τρώϊος</span>: <span class=\"gk\">Τροίη </span>(<span class=\"lex-etym gk\">-ης -ῃ -ην</span>) stands at the beginning of the line in the phrase <span class=\"gk\">Τροίῃ ἐν εὐρείῃ</span> <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Il.</span> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">24.256</span> </span>,<span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">494</span> </span>, <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-author\">Od.</span> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">1.62</span> </span>, <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">4.99</span> </span>, <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">5.307</span> </span>; in the other <span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-cite-scope\">82</span> </span> occurrences the trisyll. form is admitted by the metre, but should perh. not be restored, as disyll. <span class=\"gk\">Τροίη </span>(<span class=\"gk\">-ης</span> etc.) is almost inevitably so placed in a hexam.:—codd.<span class=\"lex-cite\"> <span class=\"lex-ci